1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,813
A magnet holds a chain of paper clips.

2
00:00:02,813 --> 00:00:07,306
The first clip is held tight, the next
one less, and the last one barely.

3
00:00:07,306 --> 00:00:10,774
In a whisper game, the message
gets weaker down the line.

4
00:00:11,174 --> 00:00:13,012
A pull that fades as it travels.

5
00:00:13,012 --> 00:00:15,012
Molecules have a pull like this too.

6
00:00:15,462 --> 00:00:17,388
Diya is cooking with vinegar.

7
00:00:17,388 --> 00:00:19,669
It has acetic acid, a weak acid.

8
00:00:20,019 --> 00:00:24,404
Her book says, swap just one hydrogen
for chlorine, and the acid becomes

9
00:00:24,404 --> 00:00:26,241
almost eighty times stronger.

10
00:00:26,241 --> 00:00:28,391
How can one atom change so much?

11
00:00:28,891 --> 00:00:32,725
By the end, you will see the pull
that makes this happen, and a second

12
00:00:32,725 --> 00:00:35,265
effect that switches on
only during a reaction.

13
00:00:35,915 --> 00:00:37,456
First, a quick reminder.

14
00:00:37,456 --> 00:00:41,590
In a bond, two atoms share a pair of
electrons, like two kids holding one

15
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:42,490
rope.

16
00:00:42,422 --> 00:00:46,799
If one atom is more electronegative,
it pulls the pair closer.

17
00:00:46,799 --> 00:00:50,355
It gets a small minus
charge, called delta minus.

18
00:00:50,706 --> 00:00:52,664
The other atom gets delta plus.

19
00:00:52,664 --> 00:00:55,701
Now that atom pulls on its
own neighbour a little.

20
00:00:56,051 --> 00:01:00,974
This pull passed along a chain of sigma
bonds is called the inductive effect.

21
00:01:01,324 --> 00:01:02,393
It is permanent.

22
00:01:02,393 --> 00:01:06,510
It is always there, even when the
molecule is just sitting in a bottle.

23
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:09,446
Let's watch it on our molecule bench.

24
00:01:09,446 --> 00:01:13,462
Here is a chain of three carbons
with a chlorine atom at the end.

25
00:01:13,812 --> 00:01:17,843
Chlorine pulls the shared pair of the
carbon chlorine bond towards itself.

26
00:01:17,843 --> 00:01:21,200
Chlorine becomes delta minus,
carbon one becomes delta plus.

27
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:25,433
Carbon one now pulls a little
on the bond to carbon two.

28
00:01:25,433 --> 00:01:29,134
Carbon two becomes double
delta plus, a smaller charge.

29
00:01:29,534 --> 00:01:31,610
Carbon three feels even less.

30
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:32,990
Triple delta plus.

31
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:36,031
The pull fades fast, like the paper clips.

32
00:01:36,431 --> 00:01:39,658
After about three bonds,
the effect is almost zero.

33
00:01:40,058 --> 00:01:42,151
Groups that pull electrons
show the minus I effect.

34
00:01:42,151 --> 00:01:44,676
Halogens, nitro, cyano, carboxy,
ester and aryloxy groups.

35
00:01:45,076 --> 00:01:48,012
Groups that push electrons
show the plus I effect.

36
00:01:48,012 --> 00:01:51,136
Alkyl groups like methyl
and ethyl are plus I groups.

37
00:01:51,786 --> 00:01:53,516
Now back to Diya's vinegar.

38
00:01:53,516 --> 00:01:56,059
Why does chlorine make the acid stronger?

39
00:01:56,059 --> 00:01:57,069
Step by step.

40
00:01:57,419 --> 00:01:58,319
Step one.

41
00:01:58,210 --> 00:02:01,706
An acid is strong if it
gives away its H plus easily.

42
00:02:01,706 --> 00:02:04,245
What is left behind is a negative ion.

43
00:02:04,995 --> 00:02:05,895
Step two.

44
00:02:05,642 --> 00:02:09,277
In chloroacetic acid, the chlorine
pulls electrons through the chain, away

45
00:02:09,277 --> 00:02:10,688
from that negative charge.

46
00:02:11,438 --> 00:02:12,338
Step three.

47
00:02:12,261 --> 00:02:16,490
The negative charge gets spread out,
like a heavy bag shared by friends.

48
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:18,653
A spread out charge is more stable.

49
00:02:19,403 --> 00:02:20,303
Step four.

50
00:02:20,271 --> 00:02:25,210
A more stable ion forms more easily,
so the acid gives H plus more easily.

51
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:26,856
It is a stronger acid.

52
00:02:27,606 --> 00:02:29,202
More chlorines pull harder.

53
00:02:29,202 --> 00:02:31,553
So trichloroacetic acid is stronger still.

54
00:02:31,553 --> 00:02:34,415
And a plus I group like
methyl does the opposite.

55
00:02:34,415 --> 00:02:35,933
It makes an acid weaker.

56
00:02:36,583 --> 00:02:38,137
Now the second effect.

57
00:02:38,137 --> 00:02:40,503
Take a molecule with a double bond.

58
00:02:40,503 --> 00:02:43,592
A double bond has a
sigma pair and a pi pair.

59
00:02:43,942 --> 00:02:48,234
When an attacking reagent comes near,
the whole pi pair shifts to one atom.

60
00:02:48,234 --> 00:02:50,102
This is the electromeric effect.

61
00:02:50,502 --> 00:02:51,402
Watch.

62
00:02:51,099 --> 00:02:53,066
An H plus comes near ethene.

63
00:02:53,066 --> 00:02:57,673
The curved arrow starts at the pi pair
and ends on the carbon where H plus

64
00:02:57,673 --> 00:02:58,573
joins.

65
00:02:58,270 --> 00:03:00,139
This is the plus E effect.

66
00:03:00,539 --> 00:03:03,909
Now a cyanide ion comes
near a CO double bond.

67
00:03:03,909 --> 00:03:08,376
The pi pair moves away from the
attacked carbon, onto the oxygen.

68
00:03:08,376 --> 00:03:10,403
This is the minus E effect.

69
00:03:10,803 --> 00:03:13,718
Take the reagent away,
and the pi pair goes back.

70
00:03:13,718 --> 00:03:15,776
The electromeric effect is temporary.

71
00:03:15,776 --> 00:03:19,729
When it acts against the inductive
effect, the electromeric effect wins.

72
00:03:20,379 --> 00:03:22,094
Compare them side by side.

73
00:03:22,094 --> 00:03:23,336
One is always on.

74
00:03:23,336 --> 00:03:26,385
The other switches on only
when a reagent arrives.

75
00:03:26,735 --> 00:03:27,797
Pause and think.

76
00:03:27,797 --> 00:03:29,697
You remove the attacking reagent.

77
00:03:29,697 --> 00:03:31,382
Which effect is still there?

78
00:03:34,582 --> 00:03:35,782
The inductive effect!

79
00:03:35,782 --> 00:03:36,779
It is permanent.

80
00:03:36,779 --> 00:03:39,683
The electromeric effect vanishes
once the reagent leaves.

81
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:41,311
Remember this.

82
00:03:41,311 --> 00:03:43,614
Inductive is a pull that never stops.

83
00:03:43,614 --> 00:03:46,237
Electromeric switches
on only for a reagent.

84
00:03:46,937 --> 00:03:48,627
Now, one worked example.

85
00:03:48,927 --> 00:03:50,524
Arrange by acid strength.

86
00:03:50,524 --> 00:03:53,525
Acetic acid, formic acid,
and chloroacetic acid.

87
00:03:54,025 --> 00:03:56,744
Chloroacetic acid has
chlorine, a minus I group.

88
00:03:56,744 --> 00:03:59,077
It pulls electrons and spreads the charge.

89
00:03:59,077 --> 00:03:59,977
Strongest.

90
00:04:00,509 --> 00:04:03,179
Formic acid has just a hydrogen there.

91
00:04:03,179 --> 00:04:04,675
No push, no pull.

92
00:04:05,425 --> 00:04:08,886
Acetic acid has a methyl
group, a plus I group.

93
00:04:08,886 --> 00:04:11,949
It pushes electrons onto
the negative charge.

94
00:04:11,949 --> 00:04:12,849
Weakest.

95
00:04:13,444 --> 00:04:17,755
So the order is chloroacetic acid,
then formic acid, then acetic acid.

96
00:04:17,755 --> 00:04:22,139
Their p K a values are about two point
nine, three point eight, and four

97
00:04:22,139 --> 00:04:23,039
point eight.

98
00:04:23,685 --> 00:04:25,149
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:25,499 --> 00:04:29,519
Some students think the inductive
effect is passed equally along the whole

100
00:04:29,519 --> 00:04:31,273
chain, however long it is.

101
00:04:31,673 --> 00:04:32,573
Wrong.

102
00:04:32,254 --> 00:04:36,558
It fades quickly with distance and
is almost nothing after three bonds.

103
00:04:36,558 --> 00:04:41,033
So a chlorine close to the acid group
makes a stronger acid than a chlorine

104
00:04:41,033 --> 00:04:41,933
far away.

105
00:04:42,464 --> 00:04:43,639
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:43,639 --> 00:04:47,449
Questions on arranging acids by strength,
sorting plus I and minus I groups,

107
00:04:47,449 --> 00:04:49,863
and comparing inductive
and electromeric effects.

108
00:04:50,462 --> 00:04:51,645
Exam level now.

109
00:04:51,645 --> 00:04:54,171
Which of these is the strongest acid?

110
00:04:54,171 --> 00:04:55,403
Pause and solve.

111
00:05:00,803 --> 00:05:02,628
The answer is B, fluoroacetic acid.

112
00:05:03,128 --> 00:05:04,837
All three halogens pull electrons.

113
00:05:04,837 --> 00:05:06,402
They show the minus I effect.

114
00:05:06,902 --> 00:05:10,990
Fluorine is the most electronegative,
so it pulls the hardest.

115
00:05:10,990 --> 00:05:15,029
The order of pull is fluorine,
then chlorine, then bromine.

116
00:05:15,529 --> 00:05:19,940
A bigger pull spreads the negative
charge more, so fluoroacetic acid is

117
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:21,008
the strongest.

118
00:05:21,008 --> 00:05:24,392
Acetic acid, with plus I
methyl, is the weakest.

119
00:05:24,992 --> 00:05:26,533
Here is your recap.

120
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:28,117
Screenshot this card.

121
00:05:31,317 --> 00:05:33,503
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:33,903 --> 00:05:34,803
One.

123
00:05:34,356 --> 00:05:37,597
Why does the inductive effect
fade along a carbon chain?

124
00:05:38,397 --> 00:05:39,297
Two.

125
00:05:38,782 --> 00:05:41,255
Why is chloroacetic acid
stronger than acetic acid?

126
00:05:42,055 --> 00:05:42,955
Three.

127
00:05:42,515 --> 00:05:45,587
What is the difference between
the plus E and minus E effects?

128
00:05:46,487 --> 00:05:50,135
Next time: how can electrons spread
around a whole ring, and even help

129
00:05:50,135 --> 00:05:51,222
from a CH bond?

130
00:05:51,222 --> 00:05:53,614
We find out in resonance
and hyperconjugation.
